Subject: virtual-fs, wasix: rename a symbolic link, and onto one, without
 following it
Last-Update: 2026-10-04

path_rename resolved both of its paths through a final symbolic link,
the host filesystem's rename asked whether its source exists by
following it, and the memory filesystem's did not find a link under its
name at all. Renaming a link whose target does not exist failed with
ENOENT, and one in a cycle with ELOOP, though rename(2) moves the link
itself; a link moved to another host directory was moved as a copy of
what it points at. A target name the runtime already knew kept its old
entry in the parent directory, so after a file was renamed onto a
symbolic link the name still resolved as the link, and a later open
followed it to wherever it used to point. The call then looked the
target up again, through that stale link, to set its name and size;
where the link pointed at nothing the lookup failed and the runtime
panicked.

The source is now looked up without following a final link, the moved
inode replaces the target's entry and takes its name, and nothing is
looked up after the rename. The host filesystem renames a link with
rename(2) in every case and leaves the times of what it points at
alone, and the memory filesystem finds a link as it finds a file, which
also has it refuse a directory created over one.

